    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but let's get a bit more granular. I need to be able to brief my clients on what this actually looks like in practice. Can you break down the specific interpersonal behaviors and even the aesthetic/presentation aspects that signal this kind of \"authority\" to a board of directors? Like, what is the specific \"vibe\" or standard they expect during a high-stakes meeting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In high-stakes meetings, commanding authority manifests as active, yet measured, listening, demonstrating that they synthesize input before responding. Interpersonally, they exhibit \"calm certainty,\" never becoming visibly flustered by critique or pressure. Aesthetically, this includes immaculate presentation, poised body language (e.g., deliberate movements), and a measured speaking pace that commands attention without needing to raise their voice. This creates a vibe of controlled competence and unflappable leadership.",
      "source": "target",
